Achieving the Smoothest Manual PIDF Tuning in PedroPathing

Manual PIDF tuning in PedroPathing is all about iterative adjustments to minimize oscillations, jerk, and overshoot while ensuring quick, stable corrections. The key to smoothness lies in starting with accurate baselines (from auto-tuners), using physical "push tests" for real-world feedback, making small incremental changes, and tuning in a logical sequence: translational (lateral stability), heading (rotation control), drive (path acceleration/deceleration), and centripetal (curve handling). This order builds stability layer by layer, as each PIDF interacts with the others. Dual PIDF systems (main for large errors, secondary for fine-tuning) add extra smoothness by handling different error scales separately. Always use the Panels dashboard for real-time adjustments—press Enter after changes to apply them instantly, and verify with multiple tests on your competition mat.

 Overall Tuning Sequence for Maximum Smoothness

1.  Complete Prerequisites: Weigh your robot for mass (in kg), set drivetrain type (e.g., mecanum), configure localizer (odometry pods with accurate offsets and encoder resolutions), and run auto-tuners for velocity/acceleration baselines. This prevents underlying issues from masking PIDF problems.
2.  Tune Translational PIDF first (lateral corrections).
3.  Tune Heading PIDF next (rotational stability).
4.  Tune Drive PIDF (forward/backward motion), with translational and heading enabled for integrated testing.
5.  Tune Centripetal Scaling last (for curves).
6.  Validate: Run a full path (e.g., Line Test OpMode) with all PIDFs on—push the robot off-course and ensure it corrects smoothly without wobble or slip.
7.  Port to Code: Update FollowerConstants with tuned values (e.g., .translationalPIDFCoefficients(new PIDFCoefficients(P, I, D, F))). Retune if hardware changes (e.g., wheel grip or weight).

Start all coefficients at 0, increment by 0.001–0.01, and prioritize feedforward (F) before P/D to overcome friction without reactive overcorrections.

 Detailed Steps for Each PIDF

 1\. Translational PIDF (Smooth Lateral Corrections)

This ensures the robot doesn't drift sideways—crucial for straight paths and overall stability.

   Setup: Open Panels, connect a gamepad (Start + A), and run Manual → Translational Tuner OpMode. The robot stays in place.
   Steps:
    1.  Set P/I/D to 0. Tune F: Increase from 0 until motors hum but the robot doesn't move (typically 0.025–0.03). This compensates static friction for smoother starts.
    2.  Tune P: Push the robot left/right (varying distances). Increase P (start at 0.01, aim for 0.06–0.1) until it corrects quickly but without overshoot. Lower if it bounces.
    3.  Tune D: Add a small value (0.001–0.005) to dampen any oscillations—robot should glide back smoothly.
    4.  I: Usually 0; add tiny (0.0001) only for persistent drift, as it can cause windup and jerk.
    5.  For dual PIDF: Tune main (coefficientsTranslationalPIDF) for large pushes first, then secondary (coefficientsSecondaryTranslationalPIDF) for fine, oscillation-free corrections.
   Smoothness Tips: Focus on push tests—aim for <1 inch final error. Ignore heading drift here (it's tuned separately). Test on slippery surfaces to force conservative gains. If using large P first, set it in the tens and D in hundredths for gentle returns.

 2\. Heading PIDF (Smooth Rotational Corrections)

This keeps orientation stable during turns or disturbances.

   Setup: Run Manual → Heading Tuner OpMode (disable auto-timer to avoid 30s stop).
   Steps:
    1.  Tune F: Same as translational—set PID to 0, increase to 0.025 until jitter starts.
    2.  Tune P: Twist the robot left/right at different angles. Increase P (0.07–0.1, higher than translational due to inertia) for snappy but non-oscillatory correction. Lower if it spins excessively.
    3.  Tune D: Low (0.002–0.005) to smooth the return—prevents wobbling.
    4.  I: Keep at 0 to avoid instability.
    5.  Dual PIDF: Main for large twists first, then secondary for precise, minimal-oscillation fixes.
   Smoothness Tips: Start conservative with P; gradual increases prevent over-rotation. Use D to fine-damp after P is set. For large P, aim 1–5 and D in hundredths—ensures fluid heading locks without jerk.

 3\. Drive PIDF (Smooth Acceleration/Deceleration Along Paths)

This controls forward/backward speed, integrating with the others for full motion.

   Setup: Run Manual → Drive Tuner OpMode—the robot moves 40 inches back/forth (adjustable in Panels). Enable translational/heading PIDFs.
   Steps:
    1.  Tune BrakingStrength (in PathConstraints): Start at 1.0; lower (0.5–0.8) for gentler stops, higher (1.2–2.0) if it undershoots. Set brakingStart 0.95 to begin decel early.
    2.  Tune F: Set others to 0, increase to 0.025 until motion begins.
    3.  Tune P (Power): Increase (0.6–1.0 for slippery wheels) for faster paths; decrease to reduce end-overshoot.
    4.  Tune D: Low (0.001) for braking smoothness.
    5.  Dual PIDF: Main first, then secondary. Use Kalman filter (T parameter in FilteredPIDFCoefficients) for noise reduction if errors jitter.
   Smoothness Tips: Observe back/forth motion—aim for even speed without skidding. Higher P risks overshoot on grippy mats; test with Line Tuner for integrated smoothness. Set zero power acceleration multiplier 2–6 (4 ideal) to balance stopping without oscillation.

 4\. Centripetal Scaling (Smooth Curves)

   Setup: Run Manual → Centripetal Tuner.
   Steps: Default 0.05; increase if robot swings wide (outside curve), decrease if it cuts inside. Tune with all PIDFs on.
   Smoothness Tips: Minimal for straight paths; focus on curve tests. Slippery wheels may need lower values for less aggressive corrections.

 General Tips for the Smoothest Results

   Incremental & Patient: Change one value at a time, test 3–5 pushes/runs per adjustment. Battery level and mat consistency matter—tune with full charge on comp surface.
   Prioritize Damping: After P, D is your smoothness hero—too little causes bounce, too much adds lag.
   Avoid I Unless Necessary: It introduces variability; skip for most FTC bots.
   Hardware Synergy: Grippier wheels (e.g., 60A durometer) allow lower gains for smoother control. Verify encoder directions early to avoid skewed tests.
   Logging & Validation: Use telemetry to log errors—target <1 inch deviation at 2–3 ft/s. If slip persists, retune velocities first.
   Common Pitfalls: Don't combine F with P/D during initial tests; enable other PIDFs only when tuning drive/centripetal. For dual systems, always large-to-small sequencing prevents abrupt transitions.

This approach should yield ultra-smooth path following—test your Bezier curve with these tunes for seamless execution! If issues arise, share bot specs for more tailored advice.